---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const scaleCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'side length a', min: 1, max: 10, step: 0.5, value: 2, unit: 'm' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'perimeter (4a)', expr: '4*a', unit: 'm' },
    { label: 'area (a²)', expr: 'a*a', unit: 'm²' },
    { label: 'volume (a³)', expr: 'a^3', unit: 'm³' },
    { label: 'volume ÷ area (a/6 surface law)', expr: 'a^3/(6*a*a)', unit: 'm' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/perimeter-area-volume">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Measurement has three dimensions and each behaves differently when things scale. Double a
    cube's side and its <strong>perimeter doubles</strong>, its <strong>area quadruples</strong>,
    its <strong>volume octuples</strong>. That mismatch — length ×1, area ×4, volume ×8 — is the
    <em>square–cube law</em>, and it quietly runs biology, engineering and cooking.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="drag the side and watch three measures drift apart — volume always wins" widget="sliderLab" config={scaleCfg} />

  <h2>Where the mismatch bites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strength scales with area, weight with volume.</strong> A mouse falls off a table and bounces; a horse falls and breaks bones. Galileo worked this out in 1638.</li>
    <li><strong>Heat loss scales with surface, heat storage with volume.</strong> Big animals stay warm; small ones eat constantly just to hold temperature.</li>
    <li><strong>Recipes don't scale linearly.</strong> Double the dish (volume ×2) needs less than double the salt and pan area behaves differently — the law in your kitchen.</li>
    <li><strong>Formulas worth knowing cold:</strong> circle C = 2πr and A = πr²; cylinder V = πr²h; sphere A = 4πr² and V = (4/3)πr³.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Notice the ratio readout: volume per unit of surface grows linearly with size. That single
    line is why cells are small, elephants have big ears, and chips have cooling problems.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Galileo, Discourses (1638), Two New Sciences; OpenStax, Prealgebra (measurement units).</p>
</TopicPage>
